Based on the Mecanum Wheel Chassis robot described in the document, let me explain the movement controls:
For Linear Velocity:

X-axis: Controls forward/backward movement

Positive values (e.g., 0.1) make the robot move forward
Negative values (e.g., -0.1) make the robot move backward
Recommended range: -0.2 to 0.2 meters per second


Y-axis: Controls sideways/lateral movement (a unique capability of Mecanum wheels)

Positive values make the robot move to the left side
Negative values make the robot move to the right side
This allows the robot to strafe sideways without changing its orientation



For Angular Velocity:

Z-axis: Controls rotational movement

Positive values (e.g., 0.1) make the robot turn counterclockwise (left)
Negative values (e.g., -0.1) make the robot turn clockwise (right)
Recommended range: -0.2 to 0.2
A value of 0 means no rotation


The angular velocity Z component creates a twisting or rotating motion. In the document, it explains that when you set a positive Z value, the robot will rotate to the left (counterclockwise when viewed from above), while a negative Z value causes it to rotate to the right (clockwise).
This combination of linear and angular velocities gives the Mecanum Wheel Chassis excellent maneuverability, allowing it to move in any direction and rotate in place - capabilities that traditional wheeled robots don't have.
